🏢 5. Active Directory Security Group Scope

Active Directory supports different security group scopes.

The three major types are:

Global
Domain Local
Universal
Global Group

A Global group is commonly used to group users or computers with similar roles or responsibilities.

Example:

Finance Users
↓
Global Group
Domain Local Group

A Domain Local group is commonly used when assigning permissions to resources within a domain.

Example:

Finance Folder
↓
Domain Local Group
↓
Permission
Universal Group

Universal groups can be used across multiple domains within an Active Directory forest.

Basic Memory
Global
↓
Group similar accounts

Domain Local
↓
Assign resource permissions

Universal
↓
Cross-domain use

At entry-level Help Desk, understanding the purpose of each scope is more important than memorizing complex group-design rules.

6. Kerberos
What is Kerberos?

Kerberos is an authentication protocol commonly used in Active Directory environments.

It uses tickets to authenticate users and services.

Basic Concept
User
 ↓
Kerberos
 ↓
Authentication Ticket
 ↓
Authorized Access

Kerberos is important because it provides a secure mechanism for authentication within a domain environment.

Interview Answer

Kerberos is an authentication protocol commonly used by Active Directory that uses tickets to securely authenticate users and services.

Help Desk Relevance

Kerberos can become relevant when troubleshooting:

Domain authentication
Access to domain resources
Authentication failures
Time synchronization problems

For first-pass learning, the ticket-based authentication concept is sufficient.

