8. Domain Trust

A trust relationship allows identities or resources in one Active Directory domain to interact with resources in another trusted domain according to configured permissions.

Concept
Domain A
    ↕
  Trust
    ↕
Domain B
Example

A company may have separate domains for different business environments but require users from one domain to access authorized resources in another.

For entry-level support, understanding the concept of cross-domain trust is sufficient.

9. Active Directory Replication

Active Directory replication synchronizes directory changes between Domain Controllers.

Example
Domain Controller 1
        ↓
   Replication
        ↓
Domain Controller 2

If a user account or group membership changes on one Domain Controller, that change may need to replicate to other Domain Controllers.

Why replication matters

It helps maintain consistent directory information across Domain Controllers.

Interview Answer

Active Directory replication synchronizes directory changes between Domain Controllers.

10. FSMO Roles

FSMO stands for:

Flexible Single Master Operations

Active Directory has five specialized FSMO roles.

These roles handle specific operations that should be performed by a single Domain Controller at a time.

First-pass understanding
FSMO
↓
Specialized Active Directory roles
↓
5 roles

For an entry-level IT Support position, detailed FSMO administration is not required at this stage.

Interview Answer

FSMO roles are specialized Active Directory roles that handle specific operations that are assigned to a single Domain Controller at a time.
